Unmanned Wingless Rotorcraft Scaling
Iuliu-Cezar Ardelean∗, Dima Usov†, Antonio Filippone‡, Ben Parslew§, Peter Hollingsworth¶ The University of Manchester, Manchester, M13 9PL, United Kingdom
The work addresses the allometric scaling of unmanned rotorcraft. Compound unmanned
rotorcraft were not considered in this study. A population of 93 unmanned rotorcraft is compiled
into a single dataset comprising size and performance parameters, including maximum take-off
weight, maximum payload weight, battery mass, battery capacity, rotor diameter, endurance,
range, maximum speed, and operational ceiling. Rotorcraft are grouped based on rotor
configuration, propulsion type, and perceived application to study the clustering of the data
points. An apparent performance limit for unmanned electric rotorcraft is found at 120 minutes
endurance and 70 kilometers range; higher range and endurance are currently only achievable
using thermal propulsion systems. Further, the regression analysis links size and performance
parameters in easy-to-use expressions for the preliminary design of unmanned rotorcraft. It is
determined that size parameters have a stronger correlation with maximum take-off weight
than performance parameters do. Comparative analysis suggests that unmanned rotorcraft
cannot be treated as a sub-class of conventional helicopters due to distinct scaling behaviors. A
comparison with theoretical models based on first-order physics principles highlights the major
role of low-Reynolds number aerodynamic effects in rotorcraft scaling.
