I. Introduction
T
he popularity of the "camera drone" (unmanned rotary-wing system for aerial photography and videography) made
the unmanned rotorcraft into a mass-produced consumer product. This success story, as well as technological
advancements in electronics components miniaturization, batteries, and autopilots, sparked the interest of industry
and academia to explore other applications where unmanned rotorcraft can be used. Consequently, there is now an
abundance of commercial off-the-shelf unmanned rotorcraft spanning several orders of magnitude in size, but their
scaling behavior has not been studied sufficiently. This work addresses the allometric scaling of unmanned rotorcraft.
2


The analysis is based on a population of commercially available rotorcraft and four prototypes of cargo wingless
multirotors for urban air mobility. The objective is to determine the regression between rotorcraft primary performance
and size parameters and contrast these with conventional rotorcraft data.
A similar paper by Lorenz [1] aimed to describe the allometric relations for fixed-wing, rotary-wing, and lighter-than
air vehicles. Lorenz compiled data on 64 rotorcraft, with a maximum take-off weight (MTOW) from 0.1 kg to 50 000 kg,
and only ten of these had a mass less than 500 kg. Lorenz collected aircraft MTOW, rotor radius, installed power, and
maximum speed. Hence, found empirical relations to describe the correlation between these. Other work in this field
was accomplished by Filippone [2] and Rand and Khromov [3], who used statistical means to describe populations of
manned rotorcraft. Although there have been follow-up studies on fixed-wing aircraft such as [4], no studies looked at
rotary-wing aircraft in the past two decades. Moreover, there has been a significant increase in the number of commercial
off-the-shelf unmanned rotorcraft since the 2010s, driven by the commercialization and affordability of "camera drones".
This study extends the allometric analysis to smaller rotorcraft scales and introduces new categories, such as endurance,
range, battery mass, battery capacity, rotor configuration, and propulsion type. The allometric relations would be
useful for preliminary design analyses, as well as studying the effect of the scale of unmanned aerial systems, e.g., for
drone-delivery networks or environmental monitoring.
A well-known study by Stolaroff et al. [5] assessed the effect of rotorcraft scale on the specific energy consumption
of drone delivery networks in support of comparison with conventional road-based transportation networks. They
used the momentum theory to model the power of two aircraft, a tetra-rotor and an octa-rotor, with "representative"
maximum payload masses of 0.5 kg and 8.1 kg, respectively. The study found that the tetra-rotor was seemingly more
suitable for drone-delivery operations and therefore recommended that drone size should be kept limited as much as
possible. However, no evidence was presented as to why the particular values that emerged from their momentum theory
model were actually representative of tetra- and octa-rotor vehicles. For example, the payload represented 19.5% of the
tetra-rotor base-case take-off weight and approximately 29.2% for the octa-rotor, respectively. Also, the base-case disk
loading was 12.5 kg m−2 for the tetra-rotor and the 20.5 kg m−2 for the octa-rotor, respectively. Therefore, this raises
the question: are the conclusions of this well-known study due to the incongruities mentioned above, or are smaller
rotary-wing aircraft actually more effective than larger ones?
Typically, rotorcraft sizing studies couple aerodynamic power modeling (e.g., using momentum theory) with
individual component scaling along the powertrain, which is decomposed into a battery, electronic speed controller,
motor, and rotor [6–16]. The coupled system takes inputs such as flight time, payload mass, and rotor radius and
iterates until the system’s MTOW converges, giving a powertrain weight breakdown. The methodology is then verified
against known aircraft configurations to estimate modeling error. The main problem with these types of studies is the
inconsistencies between the assumed scaling laws. For instance, Bershadsky et al. [8] assumed that the airframe mass
represents 19% of MTOW based on a population of a few aircraft, while Budinger et al. [12] assumed a proportion of
3


29%. This makes comparisons between studies difficult and raises questions about the veracity of the conclusions. By
contrast to typical rotorcraft studies, this paper aims to describe how rotorcraft size influences the main performance
parameters based on a statistically-significant population of unmanned rotorcraft. By extending the analysis to an
unmanned rotorcraft dataset and comparing it with conventional helicopters, scaling behavior associated with small-scale
phenomena may be revealed, the importance of which might have been neglected or poorly understood before. This
study also introduces energy-based criteria that divide the combined conventional and unmanned rotorcraft population
into clusters based on propulsion technology and rotorcraft class.
II. Methodology
A. Data Collection
A conventional helicopter database was obtained from [2] and complemented with additional categories from
[17]. Data on unmanned rotorcraft were collected from multiple sources [18–49], including rotorcraft manufacturers’
online catalogs and the Vertical Flight Society (VFS) electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL) directory [50]. No
unmanned compound rotorcraft were present in this study. The most prominent manufacturers of unmanned rotorcraft
were identified based on a web search; the online product catalogs were itemized and appended to the database according
to rotorcraft specifications sheets. In general, different manufacturers reported different performance parameters. For
example, some manufacturers reported maximum endurance at a given advance ratio [18], while others did not report
the flight regime in which the maximum endurance was achieved [34]. Where no datum was available, the entry was
left blank, increasing the database sparsity. For marketing purposes, some parameters may be exaggerated under the
assumption of ideal flight conditions (such as air temperature, wind speed, and operating altitude) and may not reflect the
actual rotorcraft performance. In the absence of a consensus within the industrial and academic rotorcraft community,
some database sparsity could not be avoided, which has also been flagged by Zhang et al. [51].
Because the database did not feature custom-built aircraft, such as those found in academic research projects, it is
expected that some variation in design performance parameters could have been lost. The data was limited to unmanned
rotorcraft since conventional helicopters are a more mature technology; instead, where applicable, this paper compares
the present work on unmanned rotorcraft with established analyses on rotorcraft allometry, such as described in Chapter
6 in [52] and in [1–3].
B. Data Grouping
The dataset comprised 93 unmanned rotorcraft described by 12 parameters. The parameters encapsulate information
about the classification, performance, and size of the rotorcraft, as defined below. Table 1 and Table 2 offer a statistical
summary of the performance and size parameters, respectively.
4


1. Classification Parameters
Rotor type: describes the rotor configuration of an aircraft, e.g., “O1“, “C4“. The first character specifies the rotor
type: “C“ for coaxial, “D“ for ducted, and “O“ for open rotors. The second character corresponds to the number of
rotors in the propulsion group. There were 93 non-empty rotor-type entries in the dataset. The modal rotor type was
“O4“, i.e., open tetra-rotor configuration (47 entries). The least frequent rotor types were coaxial hexa-rotor, coaxial
octa-rotor, and open 16-rotor configuration (1 entry each). The distribution of unmanned rotorcraft based on the rotor
type is shown in Fig. 1a.
Application: describes the perceived use of a rotorcraft. Five subcategories were identified: agricultural, consumer,
defense, educational, and enterprise. Only a fraction of rotorcraft manufacturers directly stated the intended use case. If
the use case was not present, the authors manually assigned an application entry based on manufacturer scope, the
most apparent use scenario, and vehicle acquisition costs. Commercial and educational rotorcraft were on the lower
end of the MTOW spectrum, while the defense, agricultural, and enterprise rotorcraft were on the upper end. The
most common application was enterprise (56 entries), the least common was educational (2 entries), and there were 91
non-empty entries. Figure 1b shows the distribution of the rotorcraft based on the type of perceived application.
Propulsion type: indicates whether the rotorcraft were electrically or thermally powered. The propulsion type
indicated the form of energy storage. Electrical rotorcraft used batteries, and thermal used fossil fuels as the primary
energy source. To clarify, this work classified hybrid-propulsion rotorcraft, i.e., which used fossil fuels to power electric
motors, as thermal propulsion rotorcraft. Neither gas turbines nor hydrogen fuel-cell technology were present in the
collected dataset. The propulsion-type entries were fully populated in the dataset. The database featured 16 thermal
propulsion vehicles and 77 electric. Thermal engines only appeared above the 14 kg mark, which is likely due to a
technological limit on the feasible miniaturization of that technology.
2. Performance Parameters
Endurance: represents the maximum flight time in minutes [min], subject to the size of fuel tanks or battery and the
payload configuration. Technical specifications appeared inconsistent across manufacturers, who did not usually provide
