C. Analysis Procedure
The regression analysis was performed in the MATLAB® environment using the MathWorks® native Statistics and
Machine Learning Toolbox. The primary goal of the analysis was to explore the data via clustering and regression
techniques and to extract easy-to-use empirical relations. Following that, the objective was to contrast the empirical
models with those derived from perfect allometric scaling principles, which could be considered as working hypotheses.
Perfect allometric scaling means that all volume-based properties of an object would scale with length to the power
of three, i.e., V ∝ L3, all area-based properties with length to the power of two, A ∝ L2, and mass would scale with
volume as follows, M ∝ V. In this case, volume-based properties translate to aerodynamic properties, e.g., related to
viscous effects and turbulent mixing. Assuming geometric scaling and constant rotorcraft system density, the total
actuation area, and the aircraft weight are expected to follow the square-cube law; therefore, the first hypothesis is
