Figure 4a shows the total actuation area scales proportionally to MTOW according to A = 0.13 · W0.87. The
square-cube law predicts that A ∝ W2/3; this means that the rotor area exhibits positive allometric scaling. The three
aircraft outside the 95% confidence interval are ducted tetra-rotors, as annotated in Fig. 4b, which typically have a higher
disk loading. The comparison with conventional rotorcraft, shown in Fig. 4c, suggested that conventional rotorcraft
actually have a negative allometric scaling. This is consistent with the data in Chapter 6 in [52], which attributed this
effect to nonaerodynamic constraints. In reality, it is observed that for conventional rotorcraft, the number of blades and
rotor solidity increase with vehicle size [2], which explains the negative allometry of the area with MTOW. On the
other hand, the analysis by Lorenz [1] found that, for conventional rotorcraft, the rotor area exhibits positive allometry,
A ∝ W0.80, which contradicts the findings and discussion in [2, 52].
From Fig. 4c, it can be seen that the total actuation area of unmanned rotorcraft is lower than predicted by
extrapolation of the regression analysis of the conventional helicopter population. This can be attributed to the fact
that at a small scale, the maximum available power to shaft power ratio is disproportionately large for the size of
the rotorcraft [56]. Additionally, this could also be due to the multirotor form factor and volumetric constraints that
affect multirotor more than single-rotor aircraft. Moreover, mission profiles within the visual line of sight, typical for
unmanned rotorcraft, may involve more acceleration and deceleration due to reduced operational volume, which could
10


also be a cause for unmanned rotorcraft to be overpowered. This explains an apparently steeper gradient of the area
regression curve for the unmanned population compared with the conventional population.
Figure 4d shows the disk loading variation with MTOW for unmanned and conventional rotorcraft. It can be seen
that electric rotorcraft exhibit lower disk loadings than conventional rotorcraft. This could be due to less stringent
structural constraints at smaller scales. Moreover, low Reynolds number effects at a smaller scale increase the effective
disk loading [52]. Therefore, the disk loading may be smaller by design to compensate for this performance penalty,
which is explained in more detail in Sec. III.B. Although conventional rotorcraft do not suffer from low Reynolds
number effects, structural and material constraints are a bigger concern.
B. Performance Parameters
There was no noticeable pattern between MTOW and maximum speed, as evidenced by a low coefficient of
determination, R2 = 0.23. This finding is consistent with the analysis by Lorenz [1], who attributed this phenomenon to
the high complexity of helicopters. In the case of unmanned rotorcraft, software limitations might result in an extra
layer of uncertainty and inconsistency in the data. Similarly, there was no correlation between MTOW and ceiling.
However, incorporating the maximum speed and the ceiling into kinetic and potential energies, respectively, helped
to discriminate between conventional, thermal, and electric unmanned rotorcraft, as shown in Fig. 5. This analysis
is similar to that by [1], who used maximum speed and maximum installed power in the form of k · W x · V y. The
maximum installed power was unavailable, but the maximum potential and kinetic energy were used instead, as the
maximum ceiling and the maximum velocity can be linked to the maximum available power.
