According to the results in Fig. 6a, the ideal power regression shows a good agreement with perfect allometric
scaling. However, the battery discharge power scales with MTOW according to Pe = 114 · W0.79, as shown in Fig. 6b.
This exhibits negative allometry since the second hypothesis predicted P ∝ W7/6. Simultaneously, the ratio between
13


the discharge and the ideal power, κe, is inversely proportional to MTOW, as depicted in Fig. 6c; this matches the
results in [1]. The power ratio is a measure of the electric rotorcraft’s overall efficiency. It incorporates both losses in
the electrical powertrain and non-ideal aerodynamics effects such as induced and viscous losses. The power ratio is
intended to bridge between required battery power and ideal induced power. Small-scale rotorcraft appear to consume
more power than predicted by momentum theory and geometric scaling. The power penalty is likely caused by low
Reynolds number aerodynamic effects and powertrain efficiency degradation [1, 7, 52–55, 57–59]. Another possible
reason for the power penalty is the low quality of rotor blades at small scales [60]. Although ideal power shows a good
agreement between manned and conventional rotorcraft, as shown in Fig. 6d, it is not a good metric for comparing the
two rotorcraft classes. Lorenz [1] found that the installed power of conventional helicopters scales with MTOW as
P ∝ W1.10, which is closer to perfect allometric scaling. This disparity suggests that unmanned rotorcraft cannot simply
be considered as a sub-class of full-scale conventional helicopters due to aerodynamic and technological factors.
Based on Fig. 7a, which shows the flight endurance against MTOW, two clusters are distinguishable, corresponding
to electric and thermal propulsion types. Note that the endurance was not usually measured at the MTOW reported
by the manufacturer. For electric rotorcraft, the best-fit relation between endurance and MTOW is t = 0.49 · W0.09,
which is in contrast with the third hypothesis, t ∝ W −1/6. This outcome is unexpected, also considering that the battery
mass had a negative allometric scaling with respect to MTOW, mb = 0.29 · W0.87. No comparison could be made
with conventional helicopters because the corresponding data on endurance does not exist in the literature. Figure 7b,
unmanned rotorcraft range, similarly shows two clusters for electric and thermal propulsion. The transition from electric
to thermal propulsion takes place at around 70 km range and 120 min of endurance. After this, thermal propulsion is
superior to electric propulsion by approximately one order of magnitude in terms of range and endurance. This further
evidences the difference between electric and thermal technology, highlighting the potential limitations of wingless
multirotor for urban air mobility, given the current technological level.
Range and endurance affect the suitability of an unmanned rotorcraft for a given application, yet during data collection,
it was found that these parameters were not usually reported coherently. Moreover, even when reported, confounding
factors persisted; these included unclear assumptions (e.g., safety factors, depth-of-discharge), terminology (e.g.,
maximum flight time and maximum hover time), and testing conditions (e.g., atmospheric conditions, repeatability) [51].
From a transparency perspective, it would be beneficial for the academic and industrial communities to "develop a
consensus" on measuring and reporting unmanned rotorcraft performance [51].
IV. Conclusions
