manufacturers and a lack of standardized testing methods.
The comparative analysis based on combined size and performance criteria revealed that unmanned rotorcraft
exhibit distinct allometric behavior to conventional helicopters. Hence, regression relations derived for conventional
helicopters cannot be applied to unmanned rotorcraft, suggesting that unmanned rotorcraft cannot be treated as a scaled
16


sub-class of conventional helicopters. The regression analysis indicated that the unmanned rotorcraft performance
degrades as the aircraft size decreases due to aerodynamic effects associated with low Reynolds numbers and poor
powertrain efficiency at small rotorcraft scales. The range and endurance performance of thermal propulsion rotorcraft
are superior to electrical propulsion rotorcraft. The transition between electric and thermal propulsion takes place at
around 70 km range and 120 min of endurance. This reveals a technological limit that must be addressed for large-scale
electric rotorcraft to become a competitive type of aerial vehicle.
The presented data and analysis are useful for rotorcraft engineers and aerial roboticists who aim to study the effects
of aircraft scale on a given problem, such as urban air mobility, environmental monitoring, or drone delivery services.
Future work should focus on developing novel battery technologies to fill the gap between the electric and thermal
propulsion rotorcraft. Small-scale aerodynamics should be investigated to develop mitigation strategies for the apparent
performance limitation of small rotorcraft. This could open the door for novel and exciting applications for small
rotorcraft.
